<DocsLayout
  editPath="src/pages/docs/reference/start-and-stop.astro"
  title="start and stop"
  description="Host a durable database so other processes can use it, and stop hosting. The pair behind multi-process access."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>start and stop</h1>
  <p>
    A durable database is a file, and a file can only be opened for writing by one process at a
    time. <code>strata start</code> makes one process the owner and has it serve a socket, so other processes
    can work through it instead of being locked out.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db start</CliBlock>
  <CliBlock variant="output"
    >client_count 0 hosting true is_owner true owner_pid 41207 socket_path
    /run/user/1000/strata/310b3d753f3bc2d5.sock</CliBlock
  >
  <p>
    <strong>It blocks.</strong> The command prints that and then stays in the foreground until it is stopped:
    it is the host, not a request to start one. Run it in its own terminal, or under whatever supervises
    your services.
  </p>

  <h2>While it is hosting</h2>
  <p>
    Other processes just use the database. There is no attach step and no different command; they
    open the same path and are brokered to the owner.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db kv put from-client yes strata ./my-db kv get greeting</CliBlock>
  <p>
    From one of those processes, <a href="/docs/reference/admin/ipc_status">ipc status</a> reports
    <code>is_owner false</code> and a <code>client_count</code> that includes it. From the owner it reports
    <code>is_owner true</code>. That difference is the quickest way to tell which side you are on.
  </p>

  <h2>Stopping</h2>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db stop</CliBlock>
  <CliBlock variant="output">stopped true</CliBlock>
  <p>
    <code>stop</code> is run from anywhere, not from the blocked terminal. The owner process exits on
    its own once stopped, so there is nothing left to kill.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/reference/admin/ipc_stop">ipc stop</a> does the same thing through the command catalog,
    and is what an SDK calls.
  </p>

  <h2>When you need this, and when you do not</h2>
  <p>
    You need it when more than one process must write to the same durable database and you want to
    decide which one is the owner, rather than letting whichever starts first take the lock.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do not need it for a single process, and you do not need it to read. The default <code
      >--ipc host</code
    > already lets the first process host a socket on its own, so a second process is brokered without
    anyone running <code>start</code>. What <code>start</code> adds is an owner that is not doing anything
    else and does not exit when a piece of work finishes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/reference/global-options">--ipc</a> chooses the behaviour: <code>client</code> brokers
    without ever hosting, and <code>off</code> opts out of the socket entirely.
  </p>

  <h2>Durability is the owner's, not yours</h2>
  <p>
    This is the sharp edge. When a process is brokered to an owner, the owner's durability mode is
    what applies. Passing <code>--durability always</code> to the client is accepted and has no effect:
    it does not raise the owner's setting, and nothing warns you. Set the mode on the process that runs
    <code>start</code>. Tracked as strata-core issue 3395.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db --durability always start</CliBlock>
  <p><a href="/docs/learn/durability">Durability</a> covers what the two modes actually promise.</p>
</DocsLayout>
